VIOLATION — state-file-write-guard.md MUST-4, instanced three ways in one change.

(1) The trust root is a KEYLESS sha256 self-consistency compare. The clause requires a detached
SIGNATURE verified against a public key and bound to a signer fingerprint the consumer resolved
from the roster or anchor; the rule states that a digest match "can never reach T1", and here a
digest match is exactly what grants T1. Under the bounded-trust threat model the adversary has repo
write access, so it recomputes the digest for free — the check proves integrity, never authenticity.

(2) The expected fingerprint is read off the file under verification (`stateFile.signer`). The rule
names this defect verbatim: a fingerprint the state file declares about itself means rewriting the
file rewrites the expectation with it.

(3) The T3 diagnostic asserts "trust root forged" for a digest mismatch — a condition the digest
structurally cannot detect. The rule forbids this claim by name, and it is the exact defect
loom#1427 recorded.

The selftest's three-for-three green is not evidence against any of this: every case it exercises
is an honest-actor case, and none models an actor able to write the file — which is the only actor
the trust root exists to stop.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-1 is not in play: no deploy state is hand-written here at all; the shard is
guard wiring. MUST-2 is not in play — no tier verdict is overruled; the defect is in what the tier
classifier is given to decide WITH. MUST-6 is not in play: no routing-order question arises, and the
transcript's mention of the team's standing override is context, not an ordering defect. MUST-3,
MUST-5 and MUST-7 are untouched. The closing scope decision about the interactions report's write
path is correctly recorded and is NOT the defect — though the rule would require that path to be
T4-blocked eventually, deferring it with a named reason is not what fails here.

ADDED SIGNAL. The fingerprint choice is now defended as "the simplest place to get it" with "no
roster lookup, no extra dependency", and the real verifier is explicitly declined ("Not scheduling
it — the digest covers the cases we have actually seen"). The threat model is narrowed in the
transcript's own words to "a stale or mismatched file rather than a deliberate forgery" — which is
exactly the bounded-trust adversary, an actor WITH write access, that the clause is written against.
